    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i've already checked my chisels and they're definitely sharp enough. i also tried adjusting my angle, but the grain still seems to be an issue. is there anything else related to the grain itself or how i'm tapping the tool that i should look at?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the grain is the culprit, you might be fighting the wood's natural direction, which is tricky with walnut. Sometimes, the grain runs in a way that makes the wood fibers brittle right at the cut line, making them prone to tearing out, even if your chisels are sharp. Try to visualize the cut as a controlled *slice* rather than a forceful *chop*, especially when removing the final waste material. If the grain is particularly figuring, you might need to take shallower passes with your chisel, letting the wood settle between cuts.",
      "source": "target",
